/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_Validation Validation
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Development
 * @brief Repeatable acceptance matrix for native and cross-compiled targets.
 *
 * This page defines what “the workflow works” means. A successful compilation
 * alone is not enough for a release artifact. Each supported target must pass
 * the checks that are meaningful for that target class.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationAcceptance Acceptance Criteria
 *
 * A target passes workflow validation when all applicable items below pass:
 *
 * 1. CMake accepts the preset and records the intended compiler and processor.
 * 2. `Foundation` builds independently in Debug and Release from one build tree.
 * 3. The local Debug and Release archives are different files with the correct
 *    target object format and architecture.
 * 4. One export operation installs only the Release archive, public headers,
 *    package files, and target metadata into `dist/<preset>`.
 * 5. `FoundationTargets-release.cmake` maps consumers to the Release archive,
 *    and no Debug archive or Debug import file is present in the package.
 * 6. A separate Release consumer compiles against the installed headers and
 *    links the imported target.
 * 7. Registered native executable tests pass in Debug and Release.
 *    Cross-compiled behavior is validated in real firmware or an appropriate
 *    emulator.
 * 8. Doxygen builds without unresolved workflow references.
 *
 * Record the host OS and architecture, preset, CMake version, generator,
 * compiler version, exact command, result, and artifact inspection output for
 * every run. This makes a failure reproducible instead of machine-specific.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationCurrentLimits Test-Suite Scope
 *
 * The default native configuration builds six module-scoped GoogleTest
 * executables covering Math, Utils, Containers, Functional, Time,
 * and Scheduling. CTest discovers their individual cases. A feature override
 * conditionally removes every test target whose required modules are no longer
 * all available. Always inspect the reported case count;
 * `--allow-no-tests` or `-AllowNoTests` only validates configuration and
 * compilation when zero tests are present and is not a release-test pass.
 *
 * Do not run `test.sh` or `test.ps1` as an execution test for STM32 or AVR.
 * Examples are not tests, and cross-compiled binaries cannot run directly on the
 * host. Embedded validation consists of library compilation, archive inspection,
 * consumer/firmware linkage, and target execution where hardware is available.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationPreflight Common Preflight
 *
 * Run before platform-specific checks:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * cmake --version
 * cmake --list-presets=all
 * git status --short
 * @endcode
 *
 * CMake 3.25 or newer is required by the root project. Ninja must be available
 * for every `Ninja` or `Ninja Multi-Config` preset. Confirm that the intended
 * compiler resolves before configuring; a different compiler with the same
 * executable name can create an incompatible archive.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationDesktop Desktop Matrix
 *
 * The complete Windows and macOS commands, feature variants, assertions, and
 * checkboxes live in @ref Foundation_Workflows_DesktopValidation. Keeping that
 * matrix as a subgroup prevents this acceptance contract from becoming a
 * second copy of the executable platform procedure.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationSTM32 STM32 Matrix
 *
 * Select the preset that matches the firmware CPU and floating-point ABI:
 *
 * - `stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft`;
 * - `stm32f103_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft`;
 * - `stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard`;
 * - `stm32h743_armgcc_cortex_m7f_hard`.
 *
 * The presets build a static library only. Startup code, interrupt vectors,
 * CMSIS, HAL, device definitions, memory layout, linker script, and flashing
 * remain responsibilities of the consuming STM32 firmware project.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * arm-none-eabi-gcc --version
 * arm-none-eabi-g++ --version
 * arm-none-eabi-g++ -print-sysroot
 *
 * ./scripts/clean.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard --dist
 * ./scripts/configure.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard \
 *   --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard \
 *   --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/export.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard --fresh
 * ./scripts/validate-package.sh stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard
 * @endcode
 *
 * Compile and link the exported package in the actual STM32 firmware with the
 * same `-mcpu`, `-mthumb`, `-mfloat-abi`, and `-mfpu` values. A successful
 * Foundation archive build does not prove that the final firmware uses a
 * compatible runtime or linker configuration. If configuration cannot find
 * `stdint.h`, install the target Newlib/runtime or pass a compatible
 * `FOUNDATION_ARM_SYSROOT` during a fresh configure/export.
 *
 * The exact STM32G0B1CBT6 consumer automates the package, link, and ELF checks:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test-stm32.sh \
 *     stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft \
 *     --fresh --parallel 4
 * @endcode
 *
 * The equivalent PowerShell command is:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/test-stm32.ps1 `
 *     stm32g0b1cbt6_armgcc_cortex_m0plus_soft `
 *     -Fresh -Parallel 4
 * @endcode
 *
 * Its linker map uses 128 KiB Flash at `0x08000000`. Although the MCU exposes
 * up to 144 KiB SRAM, the fixture conservatively uses the first 128 KiB at
 * `0x20000000`, which remains valid when SRAM parity protection is enabled.
 * The command does not execute the ELF.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationAVR AVR Matrix
 *
 * The initial AVR preset is exact: ATmega328P, AVR-GCC, and AVR5. Do not use its
 * archive for another AVR MCU without validating binary compatibility and adding
 * an explicit device profile.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * avr-gcc --version
 * avr-g++ --version
 * avr-g++ -mmcu=atmega328p -print-file-name=include
 *
 * ./scripts/clean.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --dist
 * ./scripts/configure.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --fresh
 * ./scripts/build.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 \
 *   --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 \
 *   --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/export.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5 --fresh
 * ./scripts/validate-package.sh atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5
 * @endcode
 *
 * The Release archive must contain `elf32-avr` objects with architecture `avr:5`.
 * Validate the installed package with a separate CMake consumer that calls a
 * non-inline Foundation function, then inspect the ELF with `avr-size` and
 * `avr-objdump`. Hardware execution remains the final check for timing, memory,
 * startup, and board integration.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationPackage Package Checks
 *
 * For every exported target, confirm these files exist:
 *
 * @code
 * dist/<preset>/include/
 * dist/<preset>/lib/<release archive>
 * dist/<preset>/lib/cmake/Foundation/FoundationConfig.cmake
 * dist/<preset>/lib/cmake/Foundation/FoundationBuildInfo.cmake
 * dist/<preset>/lib/cmake/Foundation/FoundationTargets-release.cmake
 * @endcode
 *
 * Run the identity validator for every native or cross-compiled package:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/validate-package.sh <preset>
 * @endcode
 *
 * It compares installed headers and `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake` with the
 * configured build, requires exactly one Release archive and the exact metadata
 * set, rejects Debug artifacts/imports, and inspects the real binary format,
 * architecture, and applicable floating-point ABI. A downstream Release CMake
 * project should use `find_package(Foundation CONFIG REQUIRED)` and
 * `Foundation::Foundation`; consumers should not hard-code archive names. For
 * native presets, also run the repository-owned standalone consumer after
 * export:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test-package.sh <preset> --skip-export
 * @endcode
 *
 * See @ref Foundation_Workflows_PackageConsumer for the equivalent PowerShell
 * command, exact coverage, and cross-compilation boundary.
 *
 * @section FoundationValidationReport Result Record
 *
 * Keep one record per preset with the following fields:
 *
 * @code
 * Date and operator:
 * Host OS and architecture:
 * Preset:
 * CMake / generator / compiler versions:
 * Configure result:
 * Debug build result:
 * Release build result:
 * Test result and whether tests actually executed:
 * Export result:
 * Archive architecture result:
 * External consumer or firmware link result:
 * Hardware/emulator result, if applicable:
 * Deviations, warnings, and logs:
 * @endcode
 *
 * A smoke test that used `AllowNoTests` must be recorded as “compiled, no tests
 * executed,” never as “tests passed.”
 *
